;;; An obligation's guard splits a name that starts with `or` — M1e2 **W22**,
;;; register row `W22/kw-or-in-obligation` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`),
;;; banked by S1e2.2.22 (2026-11-05) from the inventory of 2026-11-05's F1, on
;;; the user's ruling of that day (Q-M1e2.16).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two items, `a` and `b`, one fact `(orz a)`, and an obligation, `owed`,
;;; activated on `tag`: each `orz` value owes a `tag` fact.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A keyword is a whole word: `00_ebnf.md` § 1, fact 1, excludes a reserved
;;; word from `SYMBOL` only where a word boundary follows it (`neq` is not a
;;; `SYMBOL`; `neq_test` is), so `orz` is a name like any other. `a` owes a
;;; `tag` fact that no candidate pays, so every alive node owes and R is
;;; empty, owing: the answer of `kw-or-in-obligation-twin.ein`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The parser reads the guard `(orz ?a)` as the disjunction `(or z ?a)`,
;;; nested in the `:match`, which the compiler refuses: `ein solve` and `ein
;;; oracle` exit 1 with *ein.inference.compile.CompileError: nested `(or …)`
;;; in a `:match` premise: …*, a Python spelling. `ein saturate`, which reads
;;; no obligation, exits 0. (`ein solve FILE` at `35186bc0`, release and
;;; debug; every mode the same.)

(relation item Node) (relation orz Node) (relation tag Node Node)
(item a) (item b) (orz a)
(rule owed (?R)
  :match  (and (relation ?R ?A ?B) (orz ?a) (absent (?R ?a ?b)))
  :assert (open ?R))
(owed tag)
